            <ArticleTitle>The evaluation and treatment of comorbid pain and PTSD in a military setting: an overview.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>155-63</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1007/s10880-011-9236-5</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>The present article reviews the growing prevalence of comorbid pain and post-traumatic stress disorder (PTSD) in the military. This has been caused by the ongoing military conflicts in Iraq and Afghanistan, where new combat conditions/strategies are causing these comorbid conditions. Fortunately, comprehensive interdisciplinary treatment programs, originally developed for a civilian population and in academic settings, are being successfully &quot;translated&quot; or utilized in the military environment. Recent data demonstrating this translational clinical intervention are presented. Finally, challenges encountered when translating these interventions in a military environment are also discussed.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
